//! crypto/mlkem_sealed.rye -- Season G (Cryptography), the POST-QUANTUM sealed session: the ML-KEM sibling of crypto/sealed_session.rye and the
//! confidential door Comlink reaches for once the old elliptic curves fall. Where crypto/sealed_session.rye lets one keeper send another a
//! confidential message by agreeing a one-message secret with X25519, this agrees that secret with ML-KEM (FIPS 203) -- a key-encapsulation
//! mechanism, not a Diffie-Hellman, so the confidentiality it buys is the same yet hardened against the quantum day. It authors NO new
//! cryptography: it composes four primitives already GREEN -- ML-KEM (the post-quantum key agreement), SHA3-256 (the recipient-key digest bound as
//! authenticated data), BLAKE2b-512 (the key-derivation hash), and ChaCha20-Poly1305 (the authenticated encryption).
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography - Rye-native, doubled-oracle parity, audit-ready).
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260816/20260816-161537_post-quantum-mlkem-mldsa-pivot.md -- "ML-KEM is the post-quantum successor to X25519 ... the sealed
//! session (sealed_session.rye, kumara_sealed.rye) ... With this, the post-quantum sealed-session sibling of kumara_sealed.rye becomes buildable."
//! The 1,024-round itinerary, Season 3 Journey 9 (Skate) and the Comlink session surface both stand on exactly this shape, made quantum-resistant.
//! Kin: crypto/mlkem_encaps.rye + crypto/mlkem_decaps.rye (the KEM, GREEN) - crypto/mlkem_keygen.rye (the recipient keypair, GREEN) - crypto/aead.rye
//! (the seal, GREEN) - crypto/blake2b.rye (the KDF, GREEN) - crypto/sha3.rye (the ek digest, GREEN) - crypto/sealed_session.rye (its classical X25519
//! sibling) - crypto/mldsa_identity.rye (the post-quantum SIGNING identity, this one's confidentiality counterpart). Pure composition, no primitive
//! re-authored.
//!
//! THE SEALED BOX -- the standard "seal to a public key" pattern over a KEM (NaCl's crypto_box_seal shape, made post-quantum):
//!   The recipient holds a long-term ML-KEM keypair (ek, dk) and publishes ek. The sender never reuses a secret. For each message it draws a fresh
//!   32-byte KEM message m -- the caller's duty to supply fresh randomness; this library invents no entropy -- and encapsulates to the recipient's ek:
//!     (K, kem_ct) = ML-KEM.Encaps(ek, m)      [sender]      K = ML-KEM.Decaps(dk, kem_ct)      [recipient]
//!   ML-KEM's shared secret K is already bound (through the Fujisaki-Okamoto transform's G(m ‖ H(ek))) to this exact recipient and ciphertext, so
//!   a fresh m yields a fresh K every message. A single BLAKE2b-512 over (K ‖ H(ek)) yields both the 32-byte session key and the 12-byte nonce, so
//!   every message carries a fresh key AND a fresh nonce -- the AEAD nonce never reused under a key, the one rule ChaCha20-Poly1305 must keep. The
//!   recipient's own key digest H(ek) is fed to the AEAD as authenticated data, so a frame sealed to one keeper cannot be replayed as if sealed to
//!   another. The frame carries the KEM ciphertext so the recipient can recover the same secret; the sender's identity never appears (anonymous by
//!   construction), and post-quantum: an adversary who records the frame today and builds a quantum computer tomorrow still cannot read it.
//!
//! THE FRAME -- magic "GPQ1" ‖ kem-ciphertext(ct_len, fixed per parameter set) ‖ cipher-len(u64 BE) ‖ ciphertext(len) ‖ Poly1305 tag(16). open()
//! reads only public bytes, recovers K from the recipient's dk, rederives key and nonce, and returns plaintext ONLY when the AEAD tag verifies -- a
//! wrong recipient, a tampered ciphertext, a tampered tag, or a tampered KEM ciphertext all fail the same way: OpenFailed, no plaintext revealed.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device, no identity. Every keypair in the selftest is a TEST seed,
//! never a real key; the frames are held in memory, sent nowhere. The KEM message m must be fresh randomness in real use -- the selftest supplies
//! deterministic TEST values to prove the mathematics, and names that randomness duty plainly. Constant-time timing-safety stays the Season's named
//! horizon (it lives in the primitives this composes, unchanged), and agreeing a session with the maintainer's own identity key stays the custody gate.
//!
//! Run from the repository root:
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/mlkem_sealed.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const keygen = @import("mlkem_keygen.rye");
const encaps = @import("mlkem_encaps.rye");
const decaps = @import("mlkem_decaps.rye");
const aead = @import("aead.rye");
const blake2b = @import("blake2b.rye");
const sha3 = @import("sha3.rye");

pub const mlkem_sealed_version = "20260816.192000";

/// The four magic bytes opening every Grain Post-Quantum sealed frame (v1).
pub const magic: [4]u8 = .{ 'G', 'P', 'Q', '1' };

/// The 32-byte KEM message m and shared secret K widths -- FIPS 203's own, borrowed from the encaps rung.
pub const seed_bytes: u32 = keygen.seed_half_bytes; // 32
pub const shared_bytes: u32 = encaps.shared_bytes; // 32

/// Field widths, named once so seal and open agree on the layout by construction.
pub const magic_len: u32 = 4;
pub const ctlen_field_len: u32 = 8; // u64 big-endian AEAD-ciphertext length
pub const tag_len: u32 = aead.tag_bytes; // 16 — Poly1305 tag

/// One ML-KEM parameter set: the module rank k, the noise η₁, and the compression widths du, dv -- every one a FIPS 203 constant. A Params value names
/// a whole set so seal / open / keypair each cite one bundle rather than four loose numbers.
pub const Params = struct {
    k: u32,
    eta1: u32,
    du: u32,
    dv: u32,
};

/// ML-KEM-512 (NIST category 1): k=2 η₁=3 du=10 dv=4. The smallest keys and ciphertexts.
pub const mlkem_512 = Params{ .k = 2, .eta1 = 3, .du = 10, .dv = 4 };

/// ML-KEM-768 (NIST category 3): k=3 η₁=2 du=10 dv=4. The balanced default -- the set this door names for a session meant to last.
pub const mlkem_768 = Params{ .k = 3, .eta1 = 2, .du = 10, .dv = 4 };

/// ML-KEM-1024 (NIST category 5): k=4 η₁=2 du=11 dv=5. The largest keys, the highest security margin.
pub const mlkem_1024 = Params{ .k = 4, .eta1 = 2, .du = 11, .dv = 5 };

/// The default parameter set for a post-quantum sealed session: ML-KEM-768, NIST category 3, the balanced durable choice.
pub const default = mlkem_768;

/// ek_len -- the encapsulation-key (public) length for a set: 384-k + 32.
pub fn ek_len(comptime p: Params) u32 {
    // invariant (postcondition): the width is FIPS 203's ek size for this set.
    return 384 * p.k + 32;
}

/// dk_len -- the decapsulation-key (secret) length: 768-k + 96.
pub fn dk_len(comptime p: Params) u32 {
    // invariant (postcondition): the width is FIPS 203's dk size for this set.
    return 768 * p.k + 96;
}

/// ct_len -- the KEM ciphertext length: 32-du-k + 32-dv. Fixed per set, so the frame need not store it.
pub fn ct_len(comptime p: Params) u32 {
    // invariant (postcondition): the width is FIPS 203's ciphertext size for this set.
    return 32 * p.du * p.k + 32 * p.dv;
}

/// header_len -- magic ‖ kem-ciphertext ‖ ctlen-field, all fixed-width per set.
pub fn header_len(comptime p: Params) u32 {
    return magic_len + ct_len(p) + ctlen_field_len;
}

/// frame_overhead -- everything a frame carries besides the plaintext-length ciphertext body: the header and the tag.
pub fn frame_overhead(comptime p: Params) u32 {
    return header_len(p) + tag_len;
}

/// The largest plaintext a single sealed carry holds -- inherited from the AEAD's own bound, so the composition adds no looser limit.
pub const max_plain_bytes: u32 = aead.max_message_bytes; // 1 << 16

/// max_frame_bytes -- the whole frame at the largest plaintext, per set. Named so a caller's scratch cites one ceiling.
pub fn max_frame_bytes(comptime p: Params) u32 {
    return frame_overhead(p) + max_plain_bytes;
}

/// One recipient's long-term ML-KEM keypair for a set: the public ek to publish and the secret dk to keep. ek is public; dk is a secret held in the
/// caller's own jail.
pub fn KeypairType(comptime p: Params) type {
    return struct {
        ek: [ek_len(p)]u8,
        dk: [dk_len(p)]u8,
    };
}

pub const MlkemSealedError = error{
    /// seal's output buffer is too small to hold the whole frame -- refused before any byte is written.
    OutTooSmall,
    /// A plaintext longer than max_plain_bytes -- refused before any key agreement or encryption.
    PayloadTooLong,
    /// open was handed a buffer shorter than the fixed overhead, so it cannot hold even an empty-plaintext frame.
    FrameTooShort,
    /// The frame's declared ciphertext length disagrees with its actual length, or exceeds the bound -- malformed or truncated.
    LengthMismatch,
    /// The first four bytes are not the GPQ1 magic -- this is not a post-quantum sealed frame.
    BadMagic,
    /// The AEAD tag did not verify: a wrong recipient, a tampered ciphertext/tag, or a tampered KEM ciphertext. No plaintext is revealed.
    OpenFailed,
    /// A composed KEM or hash rung surfaced its own error -- honored honestly.
    ComposedFailed,
};

/// What open() returns once the tag verifies: a zero-copy slice of the recovered plaintext, which points into the caller's own out buffer.
pub const Opened = struct {
    plain: []const u8,
};

/// keypair -- grow a recipient's long-term ML-KEM keypair from two 32-byte seeds (the key seed d and the rejection secret z), composing
/// crypto/mlkem_keygen.rye. TEST seeds only -- a real recipient key reaches the custody gate.
pub fn keypair(comptime p: Params, d: *const [seed_bytes]u8, z: *const [seed_bytes]u8) MlkemSealedError!KeypairType(p) {
    var kp: KeypairType(p) = undefined;
    keygen.keygen(p.k, p.eta1, d, z, kp.ek[0..], kp.dk[0..]) catch return error.ComposedFailed;
    // invariant (postcondition): a real key was written, and the widths match the set.
    assert(!all_zero(kp.ek[0..]));
    assert(kp.ek.len == ek_len(p) and kp.dk.len == dk_len(p));
    return kp;
}

fn write_u64_be(dst: *[8]u8, v: u64) void {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 8) : (i += 1) dst[i] = @intCast((v >> @intCast(56 - 8 * i)) & 0xff);
    // invariant: the round-trip through read_u64_be recovers the value exactly.
    assert(read_u64_be(dst) == v);
}

fn read_u64_be(src: *const [8]u8) u64 {
    var v: u64 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 8) : (i += 1) v = (v << 8) | @as(u64, src[i]);
    // invariant: exactly eight bytes were folded in.
    assert(i == 8);
    return v;
}

/// The AEAD session key (32 bytes) and nonce (12 bytes), carved from one BLAKE2b-512 over the KEM shared secret and the recipient's key digest.
const Session = struct {
    key: [aead.key_bytes]u8,
    nonce: [aead.nonce_bytes]u8,
};

/// derive_session -- spread the KEM shared secret K across the whole session key and bind it to this exact recipient (via H(ek)) with one BLAKE2b-512.
/// Because K is already fresh per message (a fresh KEM message m), the derived key AND nonce are fresh per message -- the AEAD nonce is never reused.
fn derive_session(shared: *const [shared_bytes]u8, hek: *const [32]u8) Session {
    var kdf_in: [shared_bytes + 32]u8 = undefined; // K ‖ H(ek)
    var i: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): copy exactly 32 bytes of the shared secret.
    while (i < shared_bytes) : (i += 1) kdf_in[i] = shared[i];
    var j: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): copy exactly 32 bytes of the recipient key digest.
    while (j < 32) : (j += 1) kdf_in[shared_bytes + j] = hek[j];
    // The KDF input is a fixed 64 bytes, far inside BLAKE2b's bound, so the hash cannot refuse -- catch unreachable is honest.
    const h = blake2b.hash512(kdf_in[0..]) catch unreachable;
    var s: Session = undefined;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < aead.key_bytes) : (k += 1) s.key[k] = h[k];
    var n: u32 = 0;
    while (n < aead.nonce_bytes) : (n += 1) s.nonce[n] = h[aead.key_bytes + n];
    // invariant: key and nonce were carved from distinct, non-overlapping halves of the 64-byte digest.
    assert(aead.key_bytes + aead.nonce_bytes <= blake2b.digest_len);
    return s;
}

/// seal -- encapsulate a fresh secret to the recipient's ek under a fresh KEM message m (the caller's duty to supply fresh randomness), seal the
/// plaintext under the derived session, and write a whole post-quantum sealed frame into `out`, returning its length. The frame is entirely public and
/// readable only by the holder of the recipient's dk.
pub fn seal(comptime p: Params, ek: *const [ek_len(p)]u8, m: *const [seed_bytes]u8, plain: []const u8, out: []u8) MlkemSealedError!u32 {
    // invariant (precondition): the plaintext fits the AEAD's bound, so the whole frame fits and every length below stays a bounded u32.
    if (plain.len > max_plain_bytes) return error.PayloadTooLong;
    const ptlen: u32 = @intCast(plain.len);
    const frame_len: u32 = frame_overhead(p) + ptlen;
    // invariant (precondition): the caller's buffer holds the whole frame -- checked at the edge before a byte is written.
    if (out.len < frame_len) return error.OutTooSmall;

    // Encapsulate a one-message shared secret to the recipient's ek, and bind the recipient's key digest.
    var shared: [shared_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var kem_ct: [ct_len(p)]u8 = undefined;
    encaps.encaps(p.k, p.eta1, p.du, p.dv, ek[0..], m, &shared, kem_ct[0..]) catch return error.ComposedFailed;
    const hek = sha3.hash256(ek[0..]) catch return error.ComposedFailed;
    const session = derive_session(&shared, &hek);

    // --- header: magic ‖ kem-ciphertext ‖ ctlen ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < magic_len) : (i += 1) out[i] = magic[i];
    var c: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): copy exactly ct_len KEM-ciphertext bytes.
    while (c < ct_len(p)) : (c += 1) out[magic_len + c] = kem_ct[c];
    var lenbuf: [8]u8 = undefined;
    write_u64_be(&lenbuf, plain.len);
    var l: u32 = 0;
    while (l < ctlen_field_len) : (l += 1) out[magic_len + ct_len(p) + l] = lenbuf[l];

    // --- ciphertext ‖ tag: ChaCha20-Poly1305 over the plaintext, the recipient's key digest bound as authenticated data ------------------------
    const cipher = out[header_len(p) .. header_len(p) + ptlen];
    var tag: [aead.tag_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    // The plaintext is within the AEAD bound (checked above) and the aad is the 32-byte digest (within max_aad_bytes), so the seal cannot refuse for
    // length here -- catch unreachable is honest.
    aead.seal(cipher, &tag, plain, hek[0..], &session.key, &session.nonce) catch unreachable;
    var t: u32 = 0;
    while (t < tag_len) : (t += 1) out[header_len(p) + ptlen + t] = tag[t];

    // invariant (postcondition): the whole frame was written -- overhead plus ciphertext -- and nothing past it was touched.
    assert(header_len(p) + ptlen + tag_len == frame_len);
    return frame_len;
}

/// open -- recover the plaintext of a post-quantum sealed frame with the recipient's dk and ek: decapsulate the KEM ciphertext back to K, rederive the
/// session, and return the plaintext ONLY when the AEAD tag verifies. A wrong recipient, a tampered ciphertext/tag, or a tampered KEM ciphertext all
/// fail OpenFailed with no plaintext revealed.
pub fn open(comptime p: Params, dk: *const [dk_len(p)]u8, ek: *const [ek_len(p)]u8, frame: []const u8, out: []u8) MlkemSealedError!Opened {
    // invariant (precondition): the frame is at least the fixed overhead, so every fixed field below has bytes to read.
    if (frame.len < frame_overhead(p)) return error.FrameTooShort;
    if (!bytes_eq(frame[0..magic_len], magic[0..])) return error.BadMagic;

    const ctlen_u64 = read_u64_be(frame[magic_len + ct_len(p) ..][0..8]);
    if (ctlen_u64 > max_plain_bytes) return error.LengthMismatch;
    const ptlen: u32 = @intCast(ctlen_u64);
    if (frame.len != frame_overhead(p) + ptlen) return error.LengthMismatch;
    // invariant (precondition): the caller's out buffer holds the recovered plaintext, which is exactly the ciphertext length.
    if (out.len < ptlen) return error.OutTooSmall;

    // Recover the KEM ciphertext and decapsulate it back to the shared secret with the recipient's dk. A tampered KEM ciphertext yields ML-KEM's
    // implicit-rejection secret, which derives a different session and fails the AEAD tag below rather than revealing anything.
    var kem_ct: [ct_len(p)]u8 = undefined;
    var c: u32 = 0;
    while (c < ct_len(p)) : (c += 1) kem_ct[c] = frame[magic_len + c];
    var shared: [shared_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    decaps.decaps(p.k, p.eta1, p.du, p.dv, dk[0..], kem_ct[0..], &shared) catch return error.ComposedFailed;
    const hek = sha3.hash256(ek[0..]) catch return error.ComposedFailed;
    const session = derive_session(&shared, &hek);

    const cipher = frame[header_len(p) .. header_len(p) + ptlen];
    var tag: [aead.tag_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var t: u32 = 0;
    while (t < tag_len) : (t += 1) tag[t] = frame[header_len(p) + ptlen + t];

    const plain = out[0..ptlen];
    // open verifies the tag before writing plaintext; any mismatch (wrong recipient, tampered bytes) surfaces as OpenFailed.
    aead.open(plain, cipher, &tag, hek[0..], &session.key, &session.nonce) catch return error.OpenFailed;

    // invariant (postcondition): the tag verified, so the plaintext is authentic and was sealed to exactly this recipient.
    assert(frame.len == frame_overhead(p) + ptlen);
    return Opened{ .plain = plain };
}

// ---- shared helpers -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn all_zero(buf: []const u8) bool {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): read exactly buf.len bytes, one per turn.
    while (i < buf.len) : (i += 1) if (buf[i] != 0) return false;
    return true;
}

fn bytes_eq(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): read exactly a.len bytes, one per turn.
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

// ---- selftest ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Fill a buffer with a deterministic spread of test bytes -- TEST keys and TEST plaintexts only, never a real key or real randomness.
fn fill_test(buf: []u8, seed: u8) void {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < buf.len) : (i += 1) buf[i] = @as(u8, @truncate(@as(u32, i) *% (@as(u32, seed) | 1) +% @as(u32, seed) +% 7));
    if (buf.len > 0) buf[0] |= 1;
}

/// check_param -- prove the post-quantum sealed session for one parameter set, end to end: a round-trip across plaintext lengths, confidentiality,
/// sealed-box freshness, determinism, the wrong-recipient and four tamper refusals, and the doubled oracle (Zig's std.crypto decapsulating the very
/// KEM ciphertext our seal embeds back to the same shared secret -- the frame interoperates with the NIST standard). Kem is the Zig type.
fn check_param(comptime Kem: type, comptime p: Params) !void {
    var frame_buf: [max_frame_bytes(p)]u8 = undefined;
    var frame_buf2: [max_frame_bytes(p)]u8 = undefined;
    var out_buf: [max_plain_bytes]u8 = undefined;

    // TEST recipients -- B (the intended recipient) and an unrelated keeper C for the wrong-recipient test. NOT real identity keys.
    var b_d: [seed_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var b_z: [seed_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    fill_test(b_d[0..], 55);
    fill_test(b_z[0..], 111);
    const b = try keypair(p, &b_d, &b_z);
    var c_d: [seed_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var c_z: [seed_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    fill_test(c_d[0..], 200);
    fill_test(c_z[0..], 7);
    const c_kp = try keypair(p, &c_d, &c_z);

    var m: [seed_bytes]u8 = undefined; // TEST KEM message — in real use, fresh randomness per message
    fill_test(m[0..], 3);

    // 1. Round-trip across a spread of plaintext lengths, including empty and the maximum.
    const lengths = [_]u32{ 0, 1, 16, 64, 1000, 4096, max_plain_bytes };
    for (lengths) |plen| {
        var plain_buf: [max_plain_bytes]u8 = undefined;
        const plain = plain_buf[0..plen];
        fill_test(plain, @truncate(plen +% 5));
        const flen = try seal(p, &b.ek, &m, plain, frame_buf[0..]);
        if (flen != frame_overhead(p) + plen) return error.FrameLenWrong;
        const opened = try open(p, &b.dk, &b.ek, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..]);
        if (!bytes_eq(opened.plain, plain)) return error.PlainWrong;
    }

    // 2. Confidentiality: the ciphertext is NOT the plaintext.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 9);
        _ = try seal(p, &b.ek, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const cipher = frame_buf[header_len(p) .. header_len(p) + 64];
        if (bytes_eq(cipher, plain_buf[0..])) return error.CiphertextIsPlaintext;
    }

    // 3. Sealed-box freshness: two DIFFERENT KEM messages sealing the same plaintext to the same recipient produce DIFFERENT frames (fresh K -> fresh
    //    key + nonce), yet BOTH open to the same plaintext.
    {
        var m2: [seed_bytes]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(m2[0..], 77);
        var plain_buf: [128]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 13);
        const f1 = try seal(p, &b.ek, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const f2 = try seal(p, &b.ek, &m2, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf2[0..]);
        if (bytes_eq(frame_buf[0..f1], frame_buf2[0..f2])) return error.NoFreshness;
        const o1 = try open(p, &b.dk, &b.ek, frame_buf[0..f1], out_buf[0..]);
        if (!bytes_eq(o1.plain, plain_buf[0..])) return error.FreshOpen1Wrong;
        var out2: [128]u8 = undefined;
        const o2 = try open(p, &b.dk, &b.ek, frame_buf2[0..f2], out2[0..]);
        if (!bytes_eq(o2.plain, plain_buf[0..])) return error.FreshOpen2Wrong;
    }

    // 4. Determinism given a fixed KEM message: same inputs seal to byte-identical frames.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 21);
        const f1 = try seal(p, &b.ek, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const f2 = try seal(p, &b.ek, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf2[0..]);
        if (f1 != f2 or !bytes_eq(frame_buf[0..f1], frame_buf2[0..f2])) return error.DeterminismWrong;
    }

    // 5. Wrong recipient -- keeper C cannot open a frame sealed to keeper B: decaps yields a different (implicit-rejection) secret and the bound digest
    //    differs, so the tag fails OpenFailed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 31);
        const flen = try seal(p, &b.ek, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        if (open(p, &c_kp.dk, &c_kp.ek, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.WrongRecipientOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.OpenFailed) return error.WrongRecipientWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 6. Tamper the KEM ciphertext -- decaps yields the reject secret, so the derived session differs and the tag fails OpenFailed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 37);
        const flen = try seal(p, &b.ek, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        frame_buf[magic_len + 3] ^= 0x01;
        if (open(p, &b.dk, &b.ek, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.TamperKemOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.OpenFailed) return error.TamperKemWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 7. Tamper the ciphertext body -- the tag no longer verifies, OpenFailed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 41);
        const flen = try seal(p, &b.ek, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        frame_buf[header_len(p) + 3] ^= 0x01;
        if (open(p, &b.dk, &b.ek, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.TamperCipherOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.OpenFailed) return error.TamperCipherWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 8. Tamper the tag -- OpenFailed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 43);
        const flen = try seal(p, &b.ek, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        frame_buf[header_len(p) + 64 + 2] ^= 0x80;
        if (open(p, &b.dk, &b.ek, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.TamperTagOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.OpenFailed) return error.TamperTagWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 9. Bad magic and truncation.
    {
        var plain_buf: [32]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 51);
        const flen = try seal(p, &b.ek, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        frame_buf[0] = 'X';
        if (open(p, &b.dk, &b.ek, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.BadMagicOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadMagic) return error.BadMagicWrongError;
        }
        frame_buf[0] = 'G'; // restore
        if (open(p, &b.dk, &b.ek, frame_buf[0 .. frame_overhead(p) - 1], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.ShortOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.FrameTooShort) return error.ShortWrongError;
        }
        if (open(p, &b.dk, &b.ek, frame_buf[0 .. flen - 1], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.LengthOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.LengthMismatch) return error.LengthWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 10. THE DOUBLED ORACLE -- Zig's independent std.crypto decapsulates the very KEM ciphertext our seal embeds back to the same shared secret our
    //     seal used, so the frame's KEM half interoperates with the NIST standard. We recompute the (K, kem_ct) our seal produced by calling encaps
    //     directly on the same ek and m, then confirm Zig's SecretKey.decaps agrees on K.
    {
        var shared_mine: [shared_bytes]u8 = undefined;
        var kem_ct: [ct_len(p)]u8 = undefined;
        encaps.encaps(p.k, p.eta1, p.du, p.dv, b.ek[0..], &m, &shared_mine, kem_ct[0..]) catch return error.OracleFailed;
        if (all_zero(shared_mine[0..])) return error.TrivialWitness;
        const sk = Kem.SecretKey.fromBytes(b.dk[0..]) catch return error.OracleFailed;
        const k_ref = sk.decaps(kem_ct[0..]) catch return error.OracleFailed;
        if (k_ref.len != shared_bytes) return error.OracleWidthMismatch;
        if (!bytes_eq(shared_mine[0..], k_ref[0..])) return error.OracleSharedMismatch;
    }
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const mlkem = std.crypto.kem.ml_kem;

    try check_param(mlkem.MLKem512, mlkem_512);
    try check_param(mlkem.MLKem768, mlkem_768);
    try check_param(mlkem.MLKem1024, mlkem_1024);

    print("GREEN crypto-mlkem-sealed: the POST-QUANTUM sealed session authored in pure Rye — the ML-KEM sibling of crypto/sealed_session.rye and the " ++
        "confidential door Comlink reaches for once the old elliptic curves fall. It composes ML-KEM (FIPS 203 key agreement), SHA3-256 (the " ++
        "recipient-key digest bound as authenticated data), BLAKE2b-512 (the KDF), and ChaCha20-Poly1305 (authenticated encryption), authoring no new " ++
        "cryptography. The standard seal-to-a-public-key box over a KEM: the recipient holds a long-term ML-KEM keypair (ek, dk); for each message the " ++
        "sender draws a FRESH 32-byte KEM message m (the caller's duty to supply fresh randomness), encapsulates (K, kem_ct) = ML-KEM.Encaps(ek, m), " ++
        "and derives BOTH a 32-byte session key AND a 12-byte nonce from one BLAKE2b-512 over (K ‖ H(ek)) — so the AEAD nonce is never reused under a " ++
        "key, and the recipient's own key digest is bound as authenticated data so a frame sealed to one keeper cannot be replayed to another. The " ++
        "frame is magic GPQ1 ‖ kem-ciphertext ‖ cipher-len(u64 BE) ‖ ciphertext ‖ Poly1305 tag; open() recovers K from the recipient's dk and returns " ++
        "plaintext only when the tag verifies. Proven GREEN on metal at ALL THREE parameter sets (ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768 the default, ML-KEM-1024): a " ++
        "round-trip across seven plaintext lengths (empty through 64 KiB), the ciphertext provably not the plaintext, the sealed-box freshness " ++
        "property (two KEM messages yield different frames that both open to the same message), byte-identical determinism given a fixed KEM message, " ++
        "and five refusals — a wrong recipient, a tampered KEM ciphertext, a tampered ciphertext body, and a tampered tag all fail OpenFailed with no " ++
        "plaintext revealed, plus bad magic and two truncations — and THE DOUBLED ORACLE: Zig's independent std.crypto decapsulates the very KEM " ++
        "ciphertext our seal embeds back to the same shared secret, so the frame interoperates with the NIST standard. The module's own functions call " ++
        "no std.crypto; only the selftest invokes Zig as the parity oracle. Purely local over TEST keypairs — no real identity key, no network, no " ++
        "funds, no real device; the KEM message must be fresh randomness in real use, constant-time timing-safety stays the Season's named horizon, " ++
        "and agreeing a session with the maintainer's own identity key stays the custody gate. Comlink's confidential session now stands post-quantum, " ++
        "on primitives already parity-GREEN.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init_args: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init_args;
    return run_selftest();
}
